{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.LagrangeCoeffSum","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.LagrangeCoeffSum","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Lagrange"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.sum_prod_qratio_weighted","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.sum_prod_qratio_weighted","kind":"theorem","line":235,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LagrangeCoeffSum.lean#L235-L324","doc":"**The weighted `q`-ratio node sum.**\n\n`∑_{i ∈ s} (∏_{j≠i} (Q·v_i − v_j)/(v_i − v_j)) · Q·v_i/(Q·v_i − v_b) = Q^{|s|}/(Q − 1)`.\n\nThe weight is exactly what the seminormal entry contributes to the Markov condition, and the whole\nsum collapses to the top coefficient of `∏_{j ≠ b}(Q·t − v_j)`, which is `Q^{|s|−1}`.\n\n**`i = b` needs no special case.**  For `i ≠ b` the weight's denominator cancels the `j = b` factor\nof the numerator; at `i = b` that factor is absent but the weight is the constant `Q/(Q−1)`, and the\ntwo land on the same expression.  I first thought this case broke the uniform shape — it does not,\nand the appearance that it did came from including the `j = i` factor, which the ratio's numerator\ndoes not have.\n\n**`hne` is not decoration.**  Without it a term can be `0/0`: `Q·v_i = v_b` makes the ratio's\nnumerator vanish *and* the weight's denominator vanish, and the two sides then disagree.  In the\nintended application `s` indexes the rows that admit a box, where `β_b = β_i + 1` is impossible\nprecisely because row `i` admits one — so the hypothesis is discharged by addability, and the\ndegenerate case is the one the corner identity excludes by summing over addable cells only. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.sum_eq_bracket_mul_of_qratio_scaling","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.sum_eq_bracket_mul_of_qratio_scaling","kind":"theorem","line":198,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LagrangeCoeffSum.lean#L198-L224","doc":"**Branching, for any weight family whose box-addition ratio is the `q`-ratio.**\n\nIf `w i` — the weight of \"`μ` with a box added in row `i`\" — is `W`, the weight of `μ`, times the\n`i`-th `q`-ratio, then `∑_i w i = [|s|]_Q · W`.  That is the branching identity, and this is the\nform in which `sum_prod_qratio` delivers it.\n\nThe hypothesis `hw` is the one remaining input, and it is a genuine sub-hypothesis rather than a\nrestatement of the conclusion: it is the classical fact that the level-`r` principal specialisation\nis the Vandermonde ratio in the shifted parts `x_i = Q^{μ_i + r − i}`, from which the box-addition\nratio follows because adding a box to row `i` sends `x_i ↦ Q·x_i` and fixes every other `x_j`.\nEstablishing `hw` for `QBeta.WenzlWeight.schurPrincipal` is what still stands between this and\ngeneral-`r` branching for the corpus weight.  It is *not* stated here as a `sorry`.\n\n**Currency note (2026-08-30).**  This paragraph used to identify that gap with *\"the `q`-analogue of\nthe Frame–Robinson–Thrall hook-length identity relating `∏_c (1 − Q^{h(c)})` to the `x_i`\"*, tracked\non bean `qou-j7ln`.  That identity is **proved**, and the bean is `completed`:\n`SYTBetaGapVandermonde.prodHooksF_one_sub_pow_pair` gives it in `(1 − Q^{β_i − β_j})` form (as the\n`f n = 1 − Qⁿ` case of a per-row identity proved over an arbitrary commutative monoid), and\n`QHookVandermonde.prodHooksF_one_sub_pow_vandermonde` converts it to the Vandermonde `(x_i − x_j)`\nform this file's consumers take.  What remains is the *wiring* — feeding those to `hw` — not a\nmissing identity.  Recorded because reading this paragraph at face value cost a later session a\nnear-duplicate build of the whole chain. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.sum_prod_qratio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.sum_prod_qratio","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LagrangeCoeffSum.lean#L68-L196","doc":"**The `q`-ratio node sum.**\n\nFor pairwise distinct nonzero nodes `v i`, `i ∈ s`,\n\n`∑_{i ∈ s} ∏_{j ≠ i} (Q·v i − v j)/(v i − v j) = 1 + Q + ⋯ + Q^{|s|−1}`.\n\nThis is the algebraic heart of Pieri's rule at a principal specialisation.  Writing the level-`r`\nprincipal specialisation of `s_μ` as a Vandermonde ratio in `x_i = Q^{μ_i + r − i}`, the ratio\n`s_{μ+□ in row i}/s_μ` is exactly the `i`-th summand, and the identity says those ratios sum to\n`[r]_Q` — which is the branching identity.  It also explains for free why the non-addable rows may\nbe summed over harmlessly: if adding a box to row `i` would break the partition, then\n`Q·v i = v_{i−1}` and that summand is zero.\n\n**Two hypotheses are artefacts of the proof, not of the statement.**  The identity is a polynomial\nidentity and holds at `Q = 1` and at `v i = 0` too; `hQ1` and `hv0` are needed because the argument\ndivides by `Q − 1` and by `v i`.  Both hold in the intended application, where `v i` is a power of\n`Q` and `Q` is not a root of unity.\n\nProof: apply `sum_eval_div_prod_eq_coeff` twice — to `divX f` and to `divX g`, where\n`f = ∏_j (X − v j)` and `g = ∏_j (Q·X − v j)`.  The first pins the node sum of\n`1/(v i · ∏_{j≠i}(v i − v j))` (the residue at `0`); the second produces the wanted sum plus that\nsame correction, and the top coefficients `1` and `Q^{|s|}` do the rest. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.sub_one_mul_geom_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.sub_one_mul_geom_sum","kind":"theorem","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LagrangeCoeffSum.lean#L62-L66","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.sum_eval_div_prod_eq_coeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.sum_eval_div_prod_eq_coeff","kind":"theorem","line":43,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LagrangeCoeffSum.lean#L43-L58","doc":"**Coefficient extraction as a node sum.**\n\nFor a polynomial of degree less than the number of nodes,\n\n`∑_{i ∈ s} f(v i) / ∏_{j ∈ s \\ {i}} (v i − v j) = f.coeff (|s| − 1)`.\n\nProof: `f` equals its own Lagrange interpolant, and the top coefficient of the `i`-th basis\npolynomial is `∏_{j ≠ i}(v i − v j)⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mathlib.coeff_lagrangeBasis","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mathlib.coeff_lagrangeBasis","kind":"theorem","line":33,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/LagrangeCoeffSum.lean#L33-L41","doc":"The top coefficient of a Lagrange basis polynomial. ","signatureChunk":0}]}